From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: W4HO <87742609+W4HO-ham@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 1 Nov 2026 18:31:14 -0647
Subject: [PATCH] dt-bindings: gnss: add Samsung's Kepler GNSS core on SPI

The GNSS receiver of the Pixel 9a is Samsung's Kepler core inside the
S5300 modem. Its data goes over SPI 21 of the Tensor G4 (USI3 of HSI0),
and two lines pace the transfers: the core's request line (gpa5-4,
which is also its interrupt) and the host's (gpp2-3). Google's kernel
describes them with two nodes, which Google's gnssif_spi driver binds:
a "samsung,gnss-spi" device on the SPI bus and a "samsung,exynos-gnss"
node holding the two lines (kernel/devices/google/zuma,
dts/zuma-cp-s5400-phone-gnss.dtsi, included by tegu's MP board).

Document both, as the 9a's device tree will use them. The differences
from Google's nodes:

- The lines are gnss2ap-spi-gpios and ap2gnss-spi-gpios, for GPIO
  descriptors, instead of gpio_gnss2ap_spi and gpio_ap2gnss_spi. Google's
  nodes flag them GPIO_ACTIVE_LOW, but Google's driver uses raw levels
  through the legacy GPIO numbers, and both lines are active high: the
  core's request is a level-high interrupt, and the host's line idles
  low. So the binding's example says GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH.
- device,name ("KEPLER", used only in log lines) and device_node_name
  ("gnss_ipc", the name of the character device the GNSS daemon opens)
  are left out: neither describes the hardware, "device" is not a
  vendor prefix, and the driver uses fixed names instead.

The compatibles stay Google's generic names, as stock's device tree and
gnssd know the device by them. A specific compatible for the S5300's
GNSS core belongs with the single-node binding an upstream submission
would want, not with this port.

The SPI device takes Samsung's SPI controller-data for the feedback
delay; stock's is 0. Stock's swap-mode belongs to the vendor SPI
controller's node and is not part of these bindings.

Link: https://android.googlesource.com/kernel/devices/google/zuma/+/42395e8adcb880aa336f0da6bb2a19ed53929c40/dts/zuma-cp-s5400-phone-gnss.dtsi
Signed-off-by: W4HO <87742609+W4HO-ham@users.noreply.github.com>
---
 .../bindings/gnss/samsung,exynos-gnss.yaml    | 50 ++++++++++++++++++
 .../bindings/gnss/samsung,gnss-spi.yaml       | 52 +++++++++++++++++++
 2 files changed, 102 insertions(+)
 create mode 100644 Documentation/devicetree/bindings/gnss/samsung,exynos-gnss.yaml
 create mode 100644 Documentation/devicetree/bindings/gnss/samsung,gnss-spi.yaml

diff --git a/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/gnss/samsung,exynos-gnss.yaml b/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/gnss/samsung,exynos-gnss.yaml
new file mode 100644
index 000000000..6051df2ee
--- /dev/null
+++ b/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/gnss/samsung,exynos-gnss.yaml
@@ -0,0 +1,50 @@
+# SPDX-License-Identifier: (GPL-2.0-only OR BSD-2-Clause)
+%YAML 1.2
+---
+$id: http://devicetree.org/schemas/gnss/samsung,exynos-gnss.yaml#
+$schema: http://devicetree.org/meta-schemas/core.yaml#
+
+title: Samsung Kepler GNSS core, handshake lines
+
+maintainers:
+  - W4HO <87742609+W4HO-ham@users.noreply.github.com>
+
+description: |
+  The control side of the Kepler GNSS core's SPI link (samsung,gnss-spi):
+  two lines between the core and the host that pace the transfers. The
+  core raises its line while it has data for the host, and to answer the
+  host's request to write. The host raises its line to ask to write, and
+  pulses it to acknowledge the core's request before reading. The node has
+  no registers, clocks or supplies of its own.
+
+properties:
+  compatible:
+    const: samsung,exynos-gnss
+
+  gnss2ap-spi-gpios:
+    maxItems: 1
+    description:
+      Core to host request line, active high. It is also the host's
+      interrupt, so its GPIO controller must be able to raise one.
+
+  ap2gnss-spi-gpios:
+    maxItems: 1
+    description:
+      Host to core request line, active high. Low when the host is idle.
+
+required:
+  - compatible
+  - gnss2ap-spi-gpios
+  - ap2gnss-spi-gpios
+
+additionalProperties: false
+
+examples:
+  - |
+    #include <dt-bindings/gpio/gpio.h>
+
+    gnss {
+        compatible = "samsung,exynos-gnss";
+        gnss2ap-spi-gpios = <&gpa5 4 GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH>;
+        ap2gnss-spi-gpios = <&gpp2 3 GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH>;
+    };
diff --git a/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/gnss/samsung,gnss-spi.yaml b/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/gnss/samsung,gnss-spi.yaml
new file mode 100644
index 000000000..75939d415
--- /dev/null
+++ b/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/gnss/samsung,gnss-spi.yaml
@@ -0,0 +1,52 @@
+# SPDX-License-Identifier: (GPL-2.0-only OR BSD-2-Clause)
+%YAML 1.2
+---
+$id: http://devicetree.org/schemas/gnss/samsung,gnss-spi.yaml#
+$schema: http://devicetree.org/meta-schemas/core.yaml#
+
+title: Samsung Kepler GNSS core, SPI data link
+
+maintainers:
+  - W4HO <87742609+W4HO-ham@users.noreply.github.com>
+
+description: |
+  Samsung's Kepler GNSS core is part of the S5300 modem in the Google
+  Pixel 9a. It runs firmware that the host loads into the modem's shared
+  memory, and it exchanges its data with the host over an SPI bus of the
+  application processor (USI3 of HSI0 on Tensor G4), in blocks of 64
+  bytes. The core and its supplies belong to the modem; this node is only
+  the SPI device. The two lines that pace the transfers are described by
+  a samsung,exynos-gnss node.
+
+allOf:
+  - $ref: /schemas/spi/spi-peripheral-props.yaml#
+
+properties:
+  compatible:
+    const: samsung,gnss-spi
+
+  reg:
+    maxItems: 1
+
+required:
+  - compatible
+  - reg
+
+unevaluatedProperties: false
+
+examples:
+  - |
+    spi {
+        #address-cells = <1>;
+        #size-cells = <0>;
+
+        gnss@0 {
+            compatible = "samsung,gnss-spi";
+            reg = <0>;
+            spi-max-frequency = <3125000>;
+
+            controller-data {
+                samsung,spi-feedback-delay = <0>;
+            };
+        };
+    };
